standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-27T10:07:48.815Z", "request_id": "req_011CgSZ5JbwncG7KjdWUb3uj", "thinking_duration_ms": 143649}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgSZ5Js4XrASBLTjCa9v4", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Choice: candidate 4, p_best ≈ 0.6.** This is a mental estimate, not a simulation. The decisive gap over the daily \"exactly to forecast\" plan (candidate 2) is only about +150, so I can't rule out candidate 2.\n\n**What all plans have in common.** The starting stock runs out in the first days for every candidate. For product 0 the shortfall is about 43 units through day 2; for product 1 it is about 78 units through day 3. These losses are the same everywhere. From then on, cumulative stock behaves like a random walk with lost sales. If you order k × forecast, the average daily drift of stock is (k − 1.046) × forecast. Daily demand varies by about 7 units for product 0 and about 8.6 for product 1.\n\n**Effect of the multiplier** (estimates for 30 days, relative to ×1.0):\n- **×1.0.** Drift is slightly negative. Stock hovers near zero, so the total shortfall is about 45 units of each product. Holding cost is small, about 250–270 per product.\n- **×1.1.** Shortfall drops to about 14–17 units per product, but the extra purchases end up mostly as leftovers at the end.\n  - Product 0: about 59 extra units cost about 640, holding grows by about 220, and the saved shortfalls are worth about 650–700 (25 each). Roughly break-even, slightly worse than ×1.0.\n  - Product 1: about 70 extra units cost about 835, holding grows by about 240, and the saved shortfalls are worth only about 600 (21 each). About 450 worse.\n- **×1.2.** Stock keeps growing (about +3.5 and +4 units a day). Holding alone is about 950–1050 per product, and the end leftovers are lost. This is clearly the worst. So candidates 0, 1 and 7 (product 0 at ×1.2) lose. Candidate 0 trims its last orders, so it beats candidate 1, but it still trails the ×1.0 plans.\n\n**Effect of ordering frequency:**\n- **Every 2 days (candidates 4, 6).** You pay the 42 fee on 14 days instead of 27, saving about 546. The second-day portion sits one extra night, costing about 400–450 in holding. Net gain over daily ordering is about +100–200.\n- **Every 3 days (candidate 5).** Holding costs about 460 more than ordering every 2 days, and saves only about 210 in fees. On top of that, it orders ×1.1, so it loses.\n\n**Ranking:** 4 (every 2 days, ×1.0) > 2 (daily, ×1.0) > 6 ≈ 3 (×1.1) > 5 > 7 > 0 > 1."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4772, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2304, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4772}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-27T10:07:57.811Z", "request_id": "req_011CgSZ5JbwncG7KjdWUb3uj"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 2, "content_block": {"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_017aNkSu5MQb7HERzXHL2Sen", "name": "StructuredOutput", "input": {}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "m